# REVIEW.md — what to look at in a <REPOSITORY> diff

These criteria apply to **every diff**, whether a person or a model wrote it. They matter more when
a model wrote it, because the code reads plausible even when it is wrong.

<!-- ONLY IF THE PROJECT IS NOT AT THE ROOT — delete the whole block if the project IS the root -->
It lives at the repository root because the Code Review service only reads `REVIEW.md` there; it
applies to <PIECES>. Items that only hold for `<PROJECT-FOLDER>` say so.
<!-- END OF CONDITIONAL BLOCK -->

<!-- ONLY IF THE FILE ALREADY COVERED ANOTHER PROJECT — written ONCE and never repeated; before
     adding it, check it is not already there. It is an assumption, not a fact: if someone added
     items by hand, the sentence covers those too without being true, which is why the report names
     it as something to verify. references/monorepo-roots.md, "When a second Java project
     arrives". -->
Items that do not name a folder were written when this file covered only
`<FOLDER-ALREADY-COVERED>`: read them as written for that piece, and check them before applying
them to another.
<!-- END OF CONDITIONAL BLOCK -->

Anything a machine can check **is not here** — that is a sensor (linter, test, hook, CI). If an
item on this list can be automated, promote it to a sensor and delete it from here. This list is
only for what needs judgement.

## 1 · API hallucinations

- [ ] **Every method, signature and constant used actually exists** in the dependency version the
      `pom.xml` pins — not in a later one, and not in a similarly-named library.
- [ ] **Configuration keys exist and do what the diff assumes.** An invented property does not
      fail: it is silently ignored, and the default behaviour takes the blame months later.
- [ ] **No new dependency slipped in unjustified.** If the diff adds one, the PR says why what was
      already there did not suffice.

## 2 · Project fidelity

- [ ] **It solves the way this repository solves**, not the way the internet solves. Same layering,
      same naming, same error handling as the code next to it.
- [ ] **It does not introduce a second way to do something that already had one.** Two ways to read
      configuration, two to build HTTP responses, two to map entities — each doubles the cost of
      every future change.
- [ ] **It respects the module boundaries** the architecture tests protect — and if it changes
      them, it says so in the PR rather than loosening the rule.

## 3 · Tests of the spec

- [ ] **Tests verify the requirement, not what the code does.** A test written while looking at the
      implementation always passes and protects nothing.
- [ ] **Every acceptance criterion in the issue has a test covering it.** If one does not, the PR
      names which and why.
- [ ] **There is at least one edge case and one error case**, not only the happy path.
- [ ] **The tests fail when the change is reverted.** That is what separates a test from
      scaffolding; if nobody checked, check it locally before approving.

## 4 · Technical debt

- [ ] **What got worse so this could ship.** Something always does; if the PR does not name it,
      ask.
- [ ] **No new `TODO`, suppression or exception without a written reason beside it.** An exception
      without a comment is invisible debt.

## 5 · Trade-offs

- [ ] **What was decided without saying so.** A design decision that appears neither in the PR nor
      in an ADR is one nobody can revisit when it starts to hurt.
- [ ] **The rejected alternative is named.** No essay needed — one sentence on why not the other
      way.

## 6 · Run the change yourself

<!-- SUBFOLDER ONLY: THE FOLDER GOES INSIDE THE ITEM. The command names its folder, like any other
     scoped item. If the project IS the root, do NOT delete the item — it is the only one in this
     category — delete just " in `<PROJECT-FOLDER>`". With a second project this item is not
     rewritten either: a sibling bullet is appended with the other piece's command, and each one
     names its own. -->
- [ ] **Start it and use it** in `<PROJECT-FOLDER>` (`<RUN-COMMAND>`). The most skipped item and
      the one that catches most: green CI only says the tests that exist pass.

---

## How this list evolves

If you say something by hand in a review for the **third time**, that is not an author discipline
problem: it is a criterion missing from this file. Add it.

And the reverse: if an item here can be machine-checked, it stops being text and becomes a sensor —
a test, a lint rule, a hook. An automatable criterion left as prose is manual work someone repeats
on every PR.
